-- title:
--   Chart rings and power bases of unit discriminant
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative ring, $F$ a field that is an $R$-algebra, and $F'$ a field that is an algebra over both $F$ and $R$, compatibly (scalar tower), finite-dimensional and separable over $F$; all three live in a single universe. Let $B$ be a power basis of $F'$ over $F$, with generator $B.\mathrm{gen}$ and degree $B.\dim$, assume $B.\mathrm{gen}$ is integral over $R$, and assume there is a unit $d \in R^\times$ whose image in $F$ equals the discriminant of the $F$-basis $(B.\mathrm{gen}^i)_{i < B.\dim}$ of $F'$. For a subset $T$ of a field $L$ that is an $R$-algebra, $\mathrm{chartAlg}\ R\ L\ T$ denotes the $R$-subalgebra of $L$ consisting of the elements of $L$ integral over the $R$-subalgebra $R[T]$ generated by $T$. Then for every subset $S \subseteq F$ two assertions hold. First, an element $x \in F'$ is integral over the subalgebra of $F'$ generated by $R$ and the image of $S$ if and only if $x = \sum_{i < B.\dim} b_i\, B.\mathrm{gen}^i$ for some family $(b_i)$ of elements of $F$ integral over $R[S]$ (pushed into $F'$). Second, such a representation is unique: two families of elements of $\mathrm{chartAlg}\ R\ F\ S$ giving the same sum are equal.
--
--   This is the ring-theoretic statement that, when the discriminant of a power basis is a unit of the base ring, the chart ring of the larger field is free over the chart ring of the smaller one on the powers of the generator; the underlying mechanism is the classical dual-basis (trace-form) argument showing that the discriminant times an integral element lies in the subalgebra generated by the generator. It is used in the construction of integral models of modular curves, in [`ModularCurve.XHDRModelAtP.exists_iso_pullback_toBase_specMap_atkinLehner_complement_placePin`](thm.html#ModularCurve.XHDRModelAtP.exists_iso_pullback_toBase_specMap_atkinLehner_complement_placePin), where it supplies the ring half of the identification of the model over $F'$ as a base change of the model over $F$.
-- source:
